  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nDry conditions, low humidity, sunshine, and seasonable temperatures \nare expected again today and for the next several days. High \ntemperatures will be about 5 degrees below normal today only topping \nout in the upper 70s to low 80s. Locations closer to Lake Michigan \nin northwest Indiana and southwest Lower Michigan may not even get \nout of the mid 70s today. Several more seasonable and dry days are \nexpected through Friday as high pressure builds into the Great Lakes \nregion midweek. Expect highs to gradually warm by a few degrees each \nday through Friday as the airmass moderates. \n\nDangerous swimming conditions persist for southeast Lake Michigan \nbeaches for at least the first half of the day today. A Beach \nHazards Statement remains in effect for La Porte County, IN and \nBerrien County, MI through daybreak. Buoys offshore of the southeast \nshoreline show wave heights around 5 to 6 feet currently. A downward \ntrend is expected later this morning with waves diminishing to 2 to \n3 feet. Heed warnings from lifeguards and beach flags! \n\nIt will be dry at least through the first half of the day Friday; an \nincoming frontal system over the central Plains will move into the \nmid Mississippi River valley by late Friday night into Saturday, \nwith increasing chances (40-60%) for rain/storms returning to our \nforecast area. There are some signs in the long term model guidance \nthat this incoming system may stall for a few days to our south. A \nlarge upper level trough may form over the Great Lakes and down\ninto the mid Mississippi River Valley, becoming cutoff from the\nmain jet stream. Because of this possibility, have retained NBM\nPoPs which feature intermittent low chances (20-30%) for \nshowers Sunday and Monday as well as cooler temperatures with \nhighs only in the upper 70s to low 80s.",
